 #:seon.task{:name "an-interpreted-test-namespace-runs-without-its-fixtures"
             :plan "An interpreted test namespace runs without its `clojure.test/use-fixtures` (class F remainder of the 2026-10-10 interpreted tier, root's go 2026-10-10). Two gaps. First, at the agent REPL the fixture is stored on no namespace object `clojure.test/test-vars` reads (`(meta (:ns (meta var)))`); `(clojure.test/test-vars [#'fixture-probe-test])` ran the body alone (agent eval-fixes-4, default a740b1e26). The agent's `use-fixtures` is `seon.sci.lookup/agent-use-fixtures`, which alters `@sci/ns`, and the host namespace stays without metadata; but within ONE eval request (agent interp-honest-p1, default 56d2efa61, 2026-10-10) `*ns*`, `(the-ns 'ih.fix2)` and a def's `(:ns (meta #'probe-var))` are three distinct `sci.lang.Namespace` objects (identity hashes 809647484, 1666353344, 507399980) and none has metadata after `(use-fixtures :each (fn [f] (f)))`, which answered `{:clojure.test/each-fixtures (...)}`: SCI keeps one object per namespace under `[:namespaces ns :obj]` (`sci.impl.utils/namespace-object`), so a Seon step that rebuilds a namespace map without its `:obj` (a fork's regeneration, `seon.sci.eval/load-host-namespace`'s fresh `sci/create-ns`, `inherit-var-bindings!`) mints new objects; find that step first. Second, a namespace's unnamed top-level forms (`use-fixtures`, schema registration) are not program rows, so `seon.sci.eval/install-selected!` never evaluates them. Root's design: the namespace entity holds its unnamed top-level forms (no clj-kondo var or namespace definition starts at them) as an ordered list with their byte offsets, interpreted in file order with the defs; they get no identity, and write-back keeps them through the namespace. A first proof attempt (check runs f3fe095070c5 and 7887ca74bc25) evaluated `use-fixtures` then a deftest through `seon.test-support/evaluate` on a forked context: the deftest's evaluation answered \"Program row could not be installed; correct the reported acquisition evidence.\" instead of its Var, both in `seon.sci.eval-test` and in a fresh `ns`; that refusal is the first thing to read. Evaluating `use-fixtures` in a host-loaded namespace also adds the fixture to the compiled namespace's metadata. Done when an interpreted deftest runs inside its namespace's fixtures. Read `seon.fn/index!`, `seon.sci.eval/run-tests` and `seon.test.runner/run-vars!`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "install-row-is-twice-as-slow-after-the-stale-var-fix"
