 #:seon.task{:name "a-cold-base-derivation-is-proportional-to-declarations"
             :plan "Phase H9 of the evidence (section 3b). After seon.cache is emptied the SCI leg of starting an agent costs 1,056 ms, 1,787 MB and 81,241 calls (the evidence, 585852259), 3,604 of them `seon.schema/contract-references`. The base `seon.sci.eval/derive-base-ctx` builds (`seon.sci.eval/build-base-ctx`, `seon.sci.eval/acquire-program!`) is a copy of the JVM's compiled program: its necessary work is one projection lookup, one `seon.sci.eval/copy-host-var` per declaration and one `seon.instrument/instrumented` wrapper per contracted function, proportional to declarations, never to source text or whole-program reads. First attribute the cost with :seon.profile/called on the emptied cache in the test runs (the cold sample of seon.start-flow-benchmark-test/commit-to-paused-flow-costs), then remove each piece of program-size work that is not a Var copy, a wrapper or a projection lookup. Proof: this test: `derive-base-ctx` after `seon.cache/evict!` of every kind costs under 300 ms and 300 MB for the loaded program through `seon.profile/measure`, calls `seon.schema/contract-references` zero times (H10) and `seon.sci.eval/install-function-contract!` once per contracted function. Files: src/seon/sci/eval.clj, test/seon/sci/acquisition_cost_test.clj. Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-start-a-flow.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/start-a-flow-benchmark.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-program-context-is-a-value"
             :plan "Phase E2 of the evidence. `seon.sci.eval/program-context` (store, database value) answers the frozen program context of the value's program content: one `seon.cache/lookup` of kind `:seon.sci.eval/program-context` keyed [store loaded-commit (digest plan) projection-revisions dials] (empty plan: [store loaded-commit]); a miss derives on a disposable builder exactly as `seon.sci.eval/derive-base-ctx` and `seon.sci.eval/interpret-overlay!` do today, then freezes it (`sci.core/freeze`; the kernel snapshot and installed-function atoms become their values; no custody, recorder, print session or result objects). `seon.sci.eval/base-ctx` becomes a fork of it for its remaining callers, so every step loads. Retires kinds `:seon.sci.eval/base-context` and `:seon.sci.eval/overlay-context` and `derive-base-ctx`'s two swap!s. Proof: seon.sci.eval-test/no-agent-work-writes-a-program-context: take the value for a fixture branch; an agent request defns, rebinds a program Var, defs an atom and `my.program/ns-unmap!`s; every map in the value is `identical?` to before; two commits with equal program revisions answer the `identical?` value; warm lookup under 0.5 ms and 0.5 MB through `seon.profile/measure`. Files: src/seon/sci/eval.clj, test/seon/sci/eval_test.clj. Read `sci.core/fork`, `seon.sci.eval/program-changes` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-immutable-env.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-settled-program-context-equals-its-rebuild"
             :plan "Phase E5 of the evidence. A settlement that wrote program rows derives the next program context instead of transferring the writer's Var roots into a shared base: a builder forked from the current value interprets the settled rows' plan through `seon.sci.eval/interpret-overlay!` (the plan carries no ::evaluated members), is frozen and inserted under the settled commit's key, read-through so an equal program another branch built wins. Deletes `seon.sci.eval/acquire!`, `seon.sci.eval/acquired-database?`, `seon.sci.eval/loaded-program-current?`, `seon.sci.eval/program-equal`. Why derive: an SCI fn is bound to its defining context on a context-less thread (SCI fork f8a2542d, 41194667; probe result/e159d4b5702f4: a fn defined in fork A answers A's binding on a raw Thread, B's under B's context), so a transferred root would run one agent's layer inside a shared value, and a second mechanism beside the miss path could diverge from it. Proof: seon.sci.eval-test/a-settled-program-context-equals-its-rebuild: after a request defining a function, its test and a caller, the next value and a cold `program-context` of the same commit (entry removed) resolve the same symbols to declarations with equal digests and the caller answers the same value; the writer's next request calls `seon.sci.eval/derive-overlay-plan` against the loaded commit zero times; a settled fn called from a raw Thread with no context answers the value's binding, not the writer's private one; next value under 20 ms and 10 MB for a three-definition request. Files: src/seon/sci/eval.clj, test/seon/sci/eval_test.clj. Read `seon.profile/measure`, `seon.sci.eval/record-settled-rows!` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-immutable-env.md`."}
