 #:seon.task{:name "every-sci-function-call-allocates-reading-the-dynamic-context"
             :plan "Every call of an SCI function goes through the fork's `sci.impl.fns/in-defining-ctx` (`reference-code/sci`, pin 33039c6e), which reads the dynamic `sci.ctx-store/*ctx*` to tell a context-less host thread from one SCI entered. On the JVM a dynamic Var read is `clojure.lang.Var/getThreadBinding`, which allocates a map entry from the thread's binding frame on every read. That is about a tenth of interpreted `seon.id/id`'s allocation (lane sci-interop, branch-runtime note), paid by every interpreted call on a changed branch's runtime. The obvious replacement, a thread-local SCI owns, does not work as a drop-in: `bound-fn*`, `future` and core.async convey `*ctx*` through Clojure's binding frames, which a separate thread-local would not see. So the fix is a fork design, not a one-line change. Done when an interpreted call on a thread SCI entered allocates nothing for the context check, a fork row in `sci.fork-bindings-test` keeps P5/P5b/P5c (a function called on a context-less thread re-enters its context) green, and a `seon.profile/measure` of 10,000 calls of a one-line interpreted function shows the allocation gone on parent against change. Read `sci.impl.fns/in-defining-ctx`, `sci.impl.fns/executing-ctx` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-19/branch-runtime.md#the-leaf-rule-tried-and-cut-lane-runtime-leaf-2026-10-19`."}
